  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A slow moving cold front approaches tonight and \ncrosses the area Friday. \n\nAn upper level troughing pivoting over the Great Lakes region slowly\ncontinues to track eastward through Friday, before the upper low\nmoves overhead on Saturday. At the surface, this translates a slow\nmoving cold front approaching the region on later today, before slowly\nmoving overhead and stalling nearby through Friday.\n\nWe've already had some light rains across most areas earlier but more\nwill arrive this evening and it'll become widespread overnight. Since\nthere has been a lack of rain for our area the past 2 to 3 weeks,\nthe rains are rather beneficial. Rainfall amounts will vary across\nthe area with light amounts N/W (a tenth to three- quarters of \nan inch) while southern NJ and Delmarva will have higher totals \nof 1 to 2 inches. The rains are expected to occur over a long enough\nperiods to preclude any flooding concerns. A few tstms may develop\nthis evening or overnight, but the chances of this are low so slight\nwording was included in the fcst. The front stalls for a periods\nFriday morning before resuming a SE push for the afternoon. This\nwill promote the higher QPF totals for those areas. Slow improvement\nin weather conditions for Friday with mostly fair weather N/W and\nslow improvement for the Delaware Valley ny early afternoon. The\nSE areas may not see any substantial improvement by sunset.\n\nThe cold front departs offshore Friday night, with dry conditions \nreturning to much of the area. However, guidance indicates an upper-\nlow will track eastward across northern portions of the area on \nSaturday. This will lead to a lingering chance for some light rain \nnorth of I-78, with the highest chances (40-50%) across the Poconos \nand far northern NJ. By Sunday morning, the upper-low will be east \nof the area bringing a return of dry conditions to the region along \nwith cooler temperatures.",
